+## Subtopics
+
+### Cell Invisibility Cache
+A cache attached at the beginning of the volume raycasting pipeline to skip resampling and compositing in cells where all eight samples are classified as transparent, addressing remaining transparent regions not skipped by block/octree entry points [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen when the binary mask or octree does not reach down to the cell level, leaving small transparent gaps [S2, unofficial]. The cache layout uses one bit per cell, storing combined information for eight samples [S2, unofficial]. It is more efficient than transfer function lookups for large zoom factors because one bit stores info for eight samples, avoiding repeated lookups for multiple rays hitting the same cell [S2, unofficial]. The cache size is $32^3$ bits = 4096 bytes [S2, unofficial]. It is initialized with all cells visible; as rays classify cells as invisible, their bits are set [S2, unofficial]. The cache remains valid until transfer function modifications occur [S2, unofficial].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Proportional to the number of opaque voxels along the ray, rather than the total depth $Z$ [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires an additional binary mask array of size $\lceil Z/32 \rceil \times Y \times X$ integers [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Efficiently skips large transparent regions; simple to implement alongside standard alpha blending [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Requires pre-computation of the binary mask; performance degrades if the volume is dense [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Sparse volumes where large transparent regions exist [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** Dense volumes or when memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck due to mask access [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The algorithm skips transparent regions entirely, reducing the number of compositing steps to the number of opaque voxels along the ray. In the worst case (fully opaque volume), it performs $Z$ iterations, but with bit manipulation, the inner loop runs only for opaque voxels. The `Depth` function and bit clearing are $O(1)$ or $O(\log Z)$ depending on implementation, but typically optimized to $O(1)$ with hardware support [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the binary mask, which is $X \times Y \times \lceil Z/32 \rceil$ integers. For a $512 \times 512 \times 512$ volume, this is $512 \times 512 \times 16 \times 4$ bytes $\approx 16$ MB, significantly less than the raw volume data [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** The technique is well-known in volume rendering literature for accelerating alpha-blending [S1, unofficial]. It is particularly effective for sparse volumes or volumes with large transparent regions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Precise skipping of empty regions; efficient bit-level manipulation; simple integration with standard alpha-blending [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputation of the binary mask; less effective for dense volumes with few transparent regions; threshold selection can impact visual quality [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Sparse volumes, interactive rendering where performance is critical, and when the volume has large transparent regions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Dense volumes with minimal transparency, or when memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck and mask generation is expensive [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This technique is described in the context of lookup-table based volume rendering for medical data visualization [S1, unofficial]. It is noted as a well-known acceleration technique in volume rendering literature, specifically referred to as early ray termination [S1, unofficial]. The specific use of a binary mask to skip cells is attributed to the work presented in the source [S1, unofficial].
+The technique is based on the concept of early ray termination, a well-known acceleration method in volume rendering literature [S1, unofficial]. The specific use of binary masks for empty space skipping was described in the context of interactive volume rendering for medical data visualization [S1, unofficial]. The cell invisibility cache variant was introduced to handle remaining transparent regions in octree-based rendering, improving performance for large medical datasets [S2, unofficial].
 
